        "mastery": 0.0,
        "prior_failures": 3,
        "text": "Running differentiation backwards: given a derivative, find an expression it could have come from.\n\nThe answer is never a single expression, and that is the whole reason this has its own name. Differentiating removes any constant term, because a constant does not change as x changes \u2014 so x^2, x^2 + 1 and x^2 - 40 all have the same derivative, and running backwards cannot tell you which one you started from. The + C is a stand-in for a constant you cannot recover. It is not decoration: leaving it off claims you know something the question cannot tell you.\n\nAntidifferentiating reverses the power rule: raise the exponent by one, then divide by the new exponent. Because any constant differentiates to zero, a general antiderivative must end with + C \u2014 without it you have named one antiderivative out of infinitely many.\n\nAn example \u2014 not your question, and the numbers are different:\nFind the general antiderivative of 2x.\nRaise the exponent by one: x^2.\nDivide by the new exponent, 2: 2 x^2 / 2 = x^2.\nAdd the constant: x^2 + C.",
        "opening": false
      }
    },
